      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hooper was christened in 1883 by the Oregon Railway & Navigation Company after early settler Albert J. Hooper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://couleecorridor.com/index.php/trip-planner/attractions/hooper",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Downtown Hooper still boasts the original McGregor Company General Store, now used as the farm’s offices and for family events.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://couleecorridor.com/index.php/trip-planner/attractions/hooper",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Nestled in the Palouse’s rolling wheat fields, Hooper once sat on an old Route 26 that crossed the river before the highway was realigned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hooper%2C_Washington",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 126‑foot well, drilled in the 1920s and powered by a windmill and later a diesel generator, supplied livestock water until the 1970s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://couleecorridor.com/index.php/trip-planner/attractions/hooper",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Restoration efforts are breathing new life into Hooper’s historic brick store, while nearby windmills continue to pump water, echoing the town’s pioneer roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
